    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the mean value of the measures of the three interior angles of any triangle, we start by recalling a fundamental property of triangles: the sum of the interior angles of a triangle is always \\(180^\\circ\\).\n\nLet's denote the measures of the three interior angles of the triangle as \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\). According to the property mentioned, we have:\n\\[ A + B + C = 180^\\circ \\]\n\nThe mean value of these three angles is the sum of the angles divided by the number of angles, which is 3. Therefore, we calculate the mean as follows:\n\\[ \\text{Mean} = \\frac{A + B + C}{3} \\]\n\nSubstituting the sum of the angles, we get:\n\\[ \\text{Mean} = \\frac{180^\\circ}{3} = 60^\\circ \\]\n\nThus, the mean value of the measures of the three interior angles of any triangle is \\(\\boxed{60}\\).",
